      ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-4b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-4b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-4b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186235285/assets/6167471a7c36c6c72bb42fbf-solution.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-4b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $\u25b3OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{7}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{7}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-4b-\\frac{7}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-4b=-\\frac{7}{2}a-4b.$\n\nSince $a\u2226b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{7}{2}\n$$",
